1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,280
A bullet hit him in the head, and yet he flew almost five hours back to his base.

2
00:00:05,540 --> 00:00:07,740
His name was Saburō Sakai.

3
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:12,620
He was born in 1916 in Saga Prefecture on the island of Kyūshū.

4
00:00:12,820 --> 00:00:15,780
At 16 he joined the Japanese Navy.

5
00:00:16,320 --> 00:00:24,060
In 1937 he graduated from the pilot school at Tsuchiura at the top of his class and received a silver watch from the Emperor.

6
00:00:24,300 --> 00:00:27,460
From 1938 he flew missions over China.

7
00:00:27,480 --> 00:00:36,980
In the Pacific War he joined the Tainan Air Group and flew the Mitsubishi Zero, first over the Philippines, then from Rabaul on New Britain.

8
00:00:37,140 --> 00:00:42,320
On 7 August 1942 the Americans landed on Guadalcanal.

9
00:00:42,380 --> 00:00:47,280
Sakai took a group of American aircraft for fighters and attacked them from behind.

10
00:00:47,360 --> 00:00:52,340
They were Dauntless dive bombers, and their rear gunners had seen him coming.

11
00:00:52,420 --> 00:00:53,840
A bullet hit him in the head.

12
00:00:53,860 --> 00:00:57,860
His right eye was blind, the left side of his body paralysed.

13
00:00:58,020 --> 00:01:04,840
He still flew back, about 1,000 kilometres over the sea, for 4 hours and 47 minutes.

14
00:01:04,900 --> 00:01:08,500
In Rabaul he still made his report, then he collapsed.

15
00:01:08,680 --> 00:01:13,400
He was operated on in Japan, but he never saw properly with his right eye again.

16
00:01:13,580 --> 00:01:17,860
In June 1944 he flew missions again with one eye.

17
00:01:17,920 --> 00:01:24,360
Over Iwo Jima he took 15 American Hellcats for Japanese aircraft and still escaped them.

18
00:01:24,580 --> 00:01:33,400
His memoirs speak of 64 victories; Japanese records list about 28, many of them shared.

19
00:01:33,500 --> 00:01:41,240
Decades after the war he met Harold Jones, one of the rear gunners of 7 August 1942, and shook his hand.

20
00:01:41,420 --> 00:01:46,520
Saburō Sakai died in September 2000 at the age of 84.
